## What Bern's Response Will Likely Highlight

### 1. Covariate Space Representation (The Big Win)
By selecting exactly 25 sites per HUC, you are executing a **Stratified Random Sample by Environmental Gradient**. 

For a Random Forest model, this is exactly what is needed. If you did a pure random sample across the whole Borough, the massive low-elevation watersheds (like Moose River) would dominate the dataset. The model would over-learn what a "low-elevation fish habitat" looks like and completely fail to accurately predict the upper limits of occupancy (ULO) in high-elevation, steep-gradient areas like Granite Creek. Equal allocation forces the algorithm to learn the environmental splits across all elevation profiles equally.

### 2. The "Strategy 2" Data Multiplier
Because you chose the **Bottom-Up approach** (starting at the downstream connection and walking up until you hit the Last Fish Observed), **your 150 sites are not just 150 data points.**

In predictive distribution modeling, a single random point that just says "Fish Present" or "Fish Absent" isn't incredibly helpful for finding a boundary. But because your crew is walking the channel to find the exact threshold, each of your 150 sites yields:
* A continuous line of **Confirmed Presence** cells.
* A definitive **Last Fish Observed (LFO)** point feature.
* A continuous line of **Confirmed Absence** cells above the LFO up to the gradient barrier.

This means your 150 field sites will actually generate thousands of rows of pixel- or reach-level training data for the model. Bern will highly value this because it provides clean, non-overlapping threshold boundaries.

