## The "Rabbit Holes" Bern Might Point Out

While your spatial distribution is sound, a consultant looking at model calibration will likely flag two things:

### The Sample Size vs. Model Complexity Constraint
While 150 continuous stream surveys provide a lot of reach-level data, your *independent master cluster count* is still $N=150$. 

In the 2023 Southeast Alaska paper, Romey used 13 spatially explicit landscape predictors (e.g., basin area, mean elevation, valley width, precipitation). If you only have 25 master survey tracks in high-elevation zones, you might run into overfitting issues if you try to throw 15 or 20 different NetMap environmental covariates into the model at once. He may suggest narrowing your initial modeling scope to 4 or 5 primary landscape drivers (like local gradient, upstream drainage area, and elevation) to keep the model stable.

