### Why This Happened
When you dissolved the stream network by `CHAN_ID`, the mainstem channel was welded into one single, continuous line feature from its headwaters all the way to its mouth. Because that mainstem line passes completely uninterrupted through the fork junction, it doesn't have a `START` or `END` vertex at that confluenceâ€”it only has an invisible intermediate vertex. 

The tributary branch, however, has a completely different `CHAN_ID`, so it physically starts or ends at that junction. When you ran the vertices tool, it dropped a point for the tributary's endpoint, but completely ignored the mainstem because the mainstem didn't start or end there. 

To get a distinct launch point for **both** upstream branches, we must force the continuous mainstem line to physically break apart at the junction.

